    <article class="tale-body">
      <p class="rishi-introduction">Rishis appear as teachers, counsellors, ancestors and witnesses. These profiles follow particular passages in the Bhagavata Purana, the Ramayana and the Skanda Purana. The links identify direct story appearances, wider narrative settings and related cosmological reading.</p>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="narada"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>Narada <small>Guidance shaped by listening</small></h2></div><p>Narada enters Dhruva’s story when hurt has become determination. He hears what the child has endured, comes to him and first counsels a steadier response to honour and insult. Dhruva explains that the wound is still too sharp for this advice to settle in his heart. Narada then guides his resolve toward devotion to Vasudeva.</p><p>The encounter makes teaching a dialogue. Narada identifies Madhuvana beside the Yamuna and gives Dhruva a way of worship and contemplation. He later reassures the child’s anxious father, Uttanapada. The sage’s care reaches both the seeker in the forest and the family left behind.</p><p>In the passage, Dhruva describes Narada travelling with a vina for the welfare of the world. His movement between people and places offers a way into the sage’s recurring role as a guide across narratives.</p><nav class="rishi-reading-links" aria-label="Read with Narada"><a href="/dhruva.html"><small>DIRECT STORY CONNECTION</small>Dhruva’s search for the Divine →</a><a href="/shiva-parvati.html"><small>ANOTHER EXISTING STORY WITH NARADA</small>Shiva and Parvati →</a><a href="/lalita-bhandasura.html"><small>ANOTHER EXISTING STORY WITH NARADA</small>Lalita and Bhandasura →</a></nav><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#rishi-narada">Bhagavata Purana 4.8: Narada’s encounter with Dhruva →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="vashishtha"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>Vashishtha <small>Counsel within the royal household</small></h2></div><p>Vashishtha, also written Vasishtha, appears in the Ramayana as one of Dasharatha’s priests and trusted counsellors. His work joins sacred learning to the responsibilities of the royal household. When Rama is to become heir, he helps prepare the consecration and guides Rama and Sita through its preparatory observance.</p><p>The coming exile changes that expected future. During the departure, Vashishtha sees Sita wearing bark garments and protests to Kaikeyi. The full account gives him a voice of counsel within the grieving court, alongside the choices of the central characters.</p><p>Reading him beside Rama’s story draws attention to those who advise, prepare and question around a moment of crisis. The profile supplies context from the fuller departure account around the events retold in the site’s shorter exile reader.</p><nav class="rishi-reading-links" aria-label="Read with Vashishtha"><a href="/rama-exile.html"><small>WIDER RAMAYANA SETTING</small>Rama’s exile →</a></nav><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#rishi-vashishtha">Griffith’s Ramayana: the court, consecration and departure →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="atri"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>Atri <small>Contemplation and hospitality</small></h2></div><p>In the Bhagavata Purana, Atri’s account joins tapas with the continuation of a family line. With Anasuya, he goes to Rksha mountain, seeking the Lord of the universe and a son like him. Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva appear, giving the episode its central question: why have three arrived when his contemplation was directed toward one?</p><p>The three respond to his intention and promise sons through their own aspects. This chapter names Soma, Dattatreya and Durvasa, associating them respectively with Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva. These relationships belong to this particular telling.</p><p>The Ramayana offers another setting. Later in the forest journey, Atri welcomes Rama, Sita and Lakshmana to his retreat. He introduces Anasuya, whose conversation with Sita and gifts give the visit its own centre of hospitality and counsel. That encounter comes after Chitrakuta, beyond the endpoint of the site’s condensed exile story.</p><nav class="rishi-reading-links" aria-label="Read with Atri"><a href="/rama-exile.html"><small>EARLIER IN THE RAMAYANA JOURNEY</small>Rama’s exile →</a><a href="/creation-universe.html"><small>RELATED CREATION CONTEXT</small>Sarga and Visarga →</a></nav><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#rishi-atri">Atri in Bhagavata 4.1 and the Ramayana’s forest journey →</a></section>
