      <section class="tale-chapter" id="bhrigu"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>Bhrigu <small>Lineage and a difficult ritual encounter</small></h2></div><p>Bhrigu appears in the Bhagavata as a figure of lineage and ritual authority. The family account names Khyati as his wife and traces descendants through Dhata and Vidhata. One branch leads through Dhata and Mrikanda to <a href="#markandeya">Markandeya</a>, giving two of these profiles a directly stated family connection.</p><p>His role in the Daksha sacrifice also includes conflict. When angry words and curses divide the assembly, Bhrigu answers with a countercurse. Later, in his prayer to the Lord, he acknowledges the power of delusion to obscure knowledge of the self and asks for grace.</p><p>The passages bring together family descent, ritual authority and the need for deeper understanding. Their particular setting gives Bhrigu an individual character within the larger world of the rishis.</p><nav class="rishi-reading-links" aria-label="Read with Bhrigu"><a href="#markandeya"><small>FAMILY CONNECTION IN THE BHAGAVATA</small>Continue to Markandeya →</a><a href="/manus-manvantaras.html"><small>RELATED CONTEXT: RISHIS AND COSMIC PERIODS</small>The Manus and Manvantaras →</a></nav><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#rishi-bhrigu">Bhagavata 4.1, 4.2 and 4.7: family, conflict and prayer →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="markandeya"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>Markandeya <small>A witness to worlds within worlds</small></h2></div><p>Markandeya opens a doorway from a sage’s life into the scale of the cosmos. In the Skanda Purana’s Purushottama account, he survives into the waters of dissolution and searches for shelter. He encounters a banyan tree and a divine child, then enters the child’s mouth and beholds fourteen worlds, beings, rivers, mountains and cities within the cosmic egg.</p><p>When he returns, wonder becomes a question about the divine power he has witnessed. The narration is rooted in Purushottama’s sacred geography. Its conclusion also turns toward worship of Maheshvara and the sage’s conquest of Death.</p><p>Read beside the cosmology guides, this particular telling gives worlds and dissolution a narrative form. The Lokas guide follows the Bhagavata’s enumeration; Markandeya’s vision here follows the Skanda account. Their distinct textual settings remain part of the reading.</p><nav class="rishi-reading-links" aria-label="Read with Markandeya"><a href="/fourteen-lokas.html"><small>RELATED COSMOLOGY: A GUIDE TO THE WORLDS</small>The Fourteen Lokas →</a><a href="/creation-universe.html"><small>RELATED COSMOLOGY: MANIFESTATION AND WITHDRAWAL</small>The creation of the universe →</a></nav><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#rishi-markandeya">Skanda Purana, Purushottama-ksetra-mahatmya 3 →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="connections"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>Follow the connections</h2></div><p>Recurring names help a reader move between narratives. The relationship may be a teacher’s direct appearance, an episode elsewhere in a larger work, a family line or a shared cosmological subject.</p><div class="cosmology-comparison"><table><caption>Paths through the existing stories and guides</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rishi</th><th scope="col">A path to follow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Narada</th><td><a href="/dhruva.html">Dhruva</a>, <a href="/shiva-parvati.html">Shiva and Parvati</a>, and <a href="/lalita-bhandasura.html">Lalita and Bhandasura</a>: the existing readers feature Narada.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Vashishtha</th><td><a href="/rama-exile.html">Rama’s exile</a>: read the shorter episode with his counsel in the fuller departure account.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Atri</th><td><a href="/rama-exile.html">Rama’s exile</a>: the starting journey leads onward to Atri and Anasuya’s later welcome.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bhrigu</th><td><a href="#markandeya">Markandeya</a>: follow the family connection through Dhata and Mrikanda in the Bhagavata.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Markandeya</th><td><a href="/fourteen-lokas.html">The Fourteen Lokas</a> and <a href="/creation-universe.html">creation and renewal</a>: compare related cosmological subjects across their named texts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A further shared setting appears in Bhagavata 3.12.21–24: Atri, Bhrigu, Vashishtha and Narada are named among Brahma’s sons in that creation account. This offers a contextual path to the <a href="/creation-universe.html">creation guide</a> alongside their individual narratives.</p><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#rishi-connections">References and notes on the connections →</a></section>
